{
  "dom_id": "question-182820",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${5}^{2n-1}+3$ is divisible by $4$ for all $n\\in N.$\n\nLet $P(n)$ denote the following sequence of statements:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3\n$$\n\n We proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nSubstituting $n=\\,${{field-2}} into the expression ${5}^{2n-1}+3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-3}}={{field-4}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=\\,${{field-7}}.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-8}}, we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,{{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}+{5}^{2k-1}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-12}} by the inductive hypothesis, and {{field-13}} we have that {{field-14}}, \nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3$ for all {{field-15}}.",
